<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">British Raj</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Raj, Indian Raj</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The British Raj was the system of direct British crown rule over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> from 1858 to 1947, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s rule ended following the rebellion of 1857. It was headed by a viceroy and administered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-civil-service">Indian Civil Service</a>. The Raj was the centrepiece of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s independence in 1947 marked the beginning of its dissolution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/british-raj.jpg" alt="British Raj" width="320" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edinburgh Geographical Institute; J. G. Bartholomew and Sons. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Indian_Empire_1909_Imperial_Gazetteer_of_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name comes from the Hindustani rāj, meaning reign, rule or government. The system was instituted on 28 June 1858, when, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a>, the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> passed to the Crown in the person of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, who was proclaimed Empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1876. The Rebellion had shaken the British enterprise without derailing it, but the British became more circumspect and drew lessons about communication between British and Indians. The Raj extended over almost all of present-day India, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, Bangladesh and Myanmar, except small holdings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>, held by Portugal, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a>, held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Two kinds of territory made up the Raj. British India was governed directly through the Governor-General and the provinces, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, 565 of them in 1947, were ruled by indigenous princes under British paramountcy. In the princely states, external affairs, defence and most communications were under British control. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india-act-1858">Government of India Act 1858</a> changed governance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in the central government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> and in the provinces.</p><p>Railways, roads, canals, bridges and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> links were built rapidly, tying the economies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> together. By 1890 some 60,000 Indians had matriculated at the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, producing an educated professional bureaucracy, though almost all of the top civil service posts were held by Britons. The population reached 255 million in the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1881. India also saw major famines. Estimates of deaths in the Great Famine of 1876 to 1878 run from 6.1 million to 10.39 million, and historians continue to debate whether British rule accelerated or distorted the country's economic development.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A new middle class grew after 1880, and Indians took encouragement from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>'s proclamation of 1858. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a>'s partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in 1905 drew a largely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> protest, and in 1906 Muslim leaders asked the new viceroy, Lord Minto, for separate electorates, which led to the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">All-India Muslim League</a> in Dacca. About 1.4 million Indian and British soldiers of the British Indian Army served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. On 13 April 1919 troops commanded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginald-dyer">Brigadier-General Reginald Dyer</a> fired on an unarmed gathering at Jallianwala Bagh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>; the government reported 379 dead and 1,100 wounded, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> estimated three times as many dead.</p><p>In 1939 the viceroy, Lord Linlithgow, declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s behalf without consulting Indian leaders, and the Congress provincial ministries resigned in protest, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> supported the war effort and grew rapidly. In 1946 mutinies in the armed services, notably in the Royal Indian Navy, prompted the Labour government to send the Cabinet Mission. In 1947 the Raj was partitioned into two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a>, the Union of India and the Dominion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “British Raj” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>There were also directly administered Crown Colonies, the <b>Indian Raj</b> governed by a viceroy and the Indian Civil Service, and the protectorates and League of Nations mandates administered under varying degrees of oversight.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>British Raj</b> was born from the crisis of the Indian Rebellion of 1857.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It brought diverse religious and ethnic groups under a centralised state, and this model of imperial rule informed successor regimes in the region, including the <b>British Raj</b>, which adopted elements of Mughal revenue administration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 1880s Britain ruled India through the <b>British Raj</b>, established when the Crown took over the government of India from the East India Company after the Indian Rebellion of 1857.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>…in The Dual Mandate in British Tropical Africa (1922), existing rulers such as the emirs of the Sokoto Caliphate in Northern Nigeria kept their courts and taxes under British supervision, as the Indian princely states did under the <b>Raj</b>; where no suitable ruler existed, administrators appointed “warrant chiefs”.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>Most Indians were peasants, who paid the land tax on which the <b>Raj</b>’s finances rested and died in the tens of millions in the famines of the later 19th century; the communities treated as untouchable stood outside the caste order altogether, and more than 1.6 million Indians were…</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li></ul></section>
